WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.883
- You're now drumming? (laughs)

00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:06.258
You're drumming, I love that
you're family must hate you.

00:00:06.258 --> 00:00:08.730
(laughing)

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.560
- I mean that's the loudest thing ever.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.760
- Can I tell you? So
my wife calls me Tappy.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:15.593
- [Kelly] Tappy.

00:00:15.593 --> 00:00:16.773
- Hey Tappy, what ya doin' Tappy?

00:00:16.773 --> 00:00:17.606
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:17.606 --> 00:00:18.610
- So what'll happen is, you'll just

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.039
subconsciously be paradiddling like.

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:22.881
(tapping)

00:00:22.881 --> 00:00:25.068
- Well especially 'cause if
it's like a new love like-

00:00:25.068 --> 00:00:28.140
- Oh my God, it's so much fun.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.830
You know I took it up
when I left the show,

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.975
I knew that I would need
something to occupy,

00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:37.760
my brain is not a brain
that does well with-

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:38.593
- [Kelly] Sitting.

00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:39.426
- Downtime.

00:00:39.426 --> 00:00:40.259
- [Kelly] Yeah

00:00:40.259 --> 00:00:41.768
- So if I have a lot of downtime it will,

00:00:41.768 --> 00:00:43.610
it'll start out with like

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.430
you've had a really rewarding career

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.748
and end up with you failed
everyone that ever loved you.

00:00:49.748 --> 00:00:50.581
(laughing)

00:00:50.581 --> 00:00:51.700
- [John] Like it goes there
like almost, you know,

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.270
it'll start here and then just like

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:54.900
a day by myself and it just goes

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.700
- Yeah
- And I sink.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.940
So I took this up about five years ago

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.590
and a buddy of mine was a drummer.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.980
And so he gives me lessons and I at first

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.780
I went on YouTube because I really

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.840
have always in my life wanting
to interact with music.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.210
Because I feel like it's such
a beautiful language that

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.620
I don't speak you know I'm not able to...

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.770
- Yeah but you enjoy it

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.750
- And so I thought to myself

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.361
but I can definitely bang
on (beep) with sticks.

00:01:27.361 --> 00:01:28.860
(laughing)

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.130
- How hard could that be? Not knowing.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.950
- Wait, that's really hard.
Drumming is hard I've tried.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.760
- I didn't know that. I just
thought it was like (shout)

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.033
but it's really nuanced. It's really,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:47.640
you know what it is, when
you're, so much of it is

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.370
limb independence and all of these things.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:53.960
And it might be a very simple move

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.260
but if your body is not
accustomed to that independence,

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.590
if you really slow it
down and you work on it

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.806
and then it starts to click.

00:02:02.806 --> 00:02:03.639
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:03.639 --> 00:02:06.840
- That feeling you could almost feel like

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.157
a new syntax forming in your brain

00:02:09.157 --> 00:02:11.140
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- And I swear to you

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.500
it's like the opposite of death.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.108
And I swear to you it's
keeping my brain elastic.

00:02:18.108 --> 00:02:20.640
- Yeah and you're right. The slowing down

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.080
like any form of music
even studies of a kid like,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.890
it's if you slow anything
down it's easier to learn.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:28.460
It's just having the patience.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.620
Like that's why I couldn't
do piano, like vocals

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.680
for some reason when I started
sight reading like with music

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.380
I picked it up really quickly,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.620
but piano I just couldn't play Beethoven

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.640
like right off the bat, so
I was like fine I'm over it.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.010
- Not doing it.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.100
- Yeah I'm not doing
it. This isn't my forte.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.310
- Yeah, I even like, I
like the busy work of it.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.060
Like he'll send me a
sheet and just be like

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.120
just do you know 16th
notes and just bang around.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.580
But you must have known that you were,

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.080
you know, I hate to use
the phrase predetermined

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.210
but you must have felt an
affinity really early on

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.130
because you can't walk
around with a gift like that

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.900
and not realize it
probably pretty early on.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:08.733
- Well, I'm with you then.

00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:12.450
So, like I was, I mean, I know
13, isn't old by any means,

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.970
but I just mean all, I don't know.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:15.270
Every time I hear people in interviews,

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.130
they're like I've been singing
and dancing since I was two.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:18.963
(laughing)

00:03:18.963 --> 00:03:21.800
And I'm like, not me. Like
I was like, I literally,

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.550
You know, everyone sang in my small town.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.390
We all went to church, so everyone sang.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.380
- See I would've thought, for you,

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.310
even in a church choir
or something like that,

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.697
like even at eight or nine years old,

00:03:31.697 --> 00:03:34.703
if you open your mouth,
people are just going to go.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.040
- That, that was odd.
- Not really.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.830
- How'd that happen.
- No. Not really.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:41.090
- Really?
I had so many

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.140
talented singers around me. I really did.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.750
I, I'm, and I, and I know
people always say like,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.187
Oh, oh my god, you sing so well

00:03:47.187 --> 00:03:48.623
and you can do so many things.

00:03:48.623 --> 00:03:50.090
I was like, I grew up
with a lot of girlfriends

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:53.100
that could do that so it wasn't
really an abnormal thing.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.790
But you keep interviewing
me, John Stewart,

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:56.393
quit interviewing me.
- Sorry.

